QSM Direct standalone profile for stock Proxmox VE 9
===================================================

Release: 0.4.0+stock4.6, Debian 13 / amd64. The standalone profile uses official
Proxmox packages: a guarded web UI overlay and independent PVE-authorized HTTPS
signalling service. It shares the native preview's media worker, encoders and
adaptive bitrate controller. No rebuilt Proxmox debs are needed on stock PVE.
Reviewed/tested stock platform: pve-manager 9.2.21, qemu-server 9.2.11,
pve-container 6.1.14. UI activation is guarded by version/template checksums;
other reviewed versions are listed in pve-manager-index-template.sha256.

Download the matching assets from:
https://github.com/rdmitry0911/qsm-rd/releases/tag/v0.4.0-qsm-direct-20261025-stock4.6
Verify SHA256SUMS-stock4.6 before installation (sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS-stock4.6
when all listed assets have been downloaded). Do not mix QSM package versions.
Do not install patched Proxmox debs from the older native preview for this setup.
The separate qsm-console-guest asset belongs INSIDE an LXC, never on the PVE host.

For signed APT installation and subsequent updates, follow:
https://rdmitry0911.github.io/qsm-rd/
The repository setup uses a scoped signing key; then install
qsm-archive-keyring and qsm-pve-direct-standalone. It distributes the same
stock4.6 debs, without patched Proxmox packages. The local-deb commands below
remain available for an offline/manual installation.

Choose the network profile
--------------------------
A. Direct UDP: PVE HTTPS 8006, a configurable QSM HTTPS port and negotiated UDP
   from browser to node. This is the normal low-latency profile.
B. Optional single-port gateway: PVE HTTPS, QSM signalling and authenticated
   TURN/TCP share TCP 8006. Use this for a firewall or router/NAT forwarding only
   that TCP port. Follow INSTALL-GATEWAY.txt / README.gateway after installing
   standalone. No public UDP or extra signalling port is needed in this mode.
   The gateway is experimental, single-node and enabled explicitly.

Install base packages as root on the PVE node:

    apt install ./qsm-pve-direct_0.4.0+stock4.6_amd64.deb \
                ./qsm-pve-direct-standalone_0.4.0+stock4.6_all.deb
    qsm-pve-direct-endpoint --show
    qsm-pve-direct-ui status
    systemctl is-active qsm-pve-direct-terminal qsm-pve-direct-signal

The UI status should be active. Hard-reload the PVE page (Ctrl+F5). Installation
starts the QSM services; it does not enable the gateway or rewrite guest configs.

Direct-UDP endpoint settings
---------------------------
First installation chooses an available IPv4 port in 18007..18106, starting
at 18007, and saves /etc/qsm-pve-direct/signal.json. Port 8007 is never selected
by standalone initialization. Existing saved config is preserved on upgrade;
older core-only installations without config retain their legacy port 8007.
The service and UI read the same config. Reload PVE after changing it.

    qsm-pve-direct-endpoint --listen 192.0.2.10 --port 18009

Use 0.0.0.0 for all IPv4 interfaces, or :: for a dual-stack IPv6 listener.
By default the browser uses the PVE page's hostname with the configured port.
For a different externally reachable signalling origin:

    qsm-pve-direct-endpoint --listen 0.0.0.0 --port 18009 \
        --public-url https://pve.example.org:28009

This setting changes signalling only; it does not relay WebRTC media or solve
media NAT traversal. For only one forwarded TCP port use the gateway instead.
The advertised HTTPS address must route to this service and match the node's
certificate. --public-url auto restores the PVE page hostname. A self-signed
certificate can require a separate exception at the signalling origin.

Allow the selected HTTPS port and negotiated WebRTC UDP from operator networks.
Changing an occupied bind address/port fails before modifying config. The CLI
reconciles the UI and restarts signalling, restoring config on failure. Existing
consoles may need reconnecting. While the gateway is enabled, this endpoint CLI
refuses binding changes: disable the gateway first.

VM setup on stock PVE
---------------------
Use root@pam to configure a VM's QSM Display1 (PVE reserves the args field for
root). Stop the selected VM, open Hardware -> Display -> Advanced and enable
QSM Display1. Choose CPU or optional VirGL, save, start the VM and open Console.
A changed display needs a full stop/start, not just an in-guest reboot.

CPU Display1 keeps the selected Standard VGA, non-GL VirtIO or VMware adapter
and noVNC. VirGL uses a private VirtIO-GPU with PVE Graphic card None; QEMU
cannot offer VNC/SPICE alongside this GL D-Bus display. For sound/microphone,
add Hardware -> Audio Device -> backend QSM Console. The optional desktop agent
provides clipboard integration. Viewing uses VM.Console; media settings writes
require VM.Config.Options. The container helper is not installed inside VMs.

Standalone owns the private per-VM D-Bus. Stock qemu-server has no dbus-display
key or native webrtcproxy/webrtcsettings API. Do not run qsm-pve-direct-migrate:
that tool converts private displays TO the patched native platform.

LXC setup
---------
Install the matching helper on the HOST:

    apt install ./qsm-pve-direct-lxc_0.4.0+stock4.6_all.deb
    qsm-pve-direct-lxc enable VMID --session wayland

Replace VMID with the chosen Debian/Ubuntu container with a Plasma desktop.
The helper embeds the matching guest deb and installs it INSIDE that container;
no separate manual guest-package installation is needed for this path. Inspect:

    qsm-pve-direct-lxc status VMID

Open the container's Console split menu -> QSM Direct. Its ordinary terminal
console remains available. Read the helper/guest docs for GPU/session setup.
The default QSM greeter is distinct from the optional original SDDM greeter.
Native SDDM and a local cursor retain their optional SDDM/Sway/kernel requirements;
see README.native-sddm and the existing Sway 1.11/wlroots instructions. Native
SDDM setup can restart container sessions; it is not performed by this release
upgrade. The SDDM term 'native' does not require patched Proxmox packages.

Upgrading an existing installation
---------------------------------
Update the core AND each installed version-matched QSM profile/helper in the
same apt transaction. For a standalone node with gateway and LXC helper:

    apt install ./qsm-pve-direct_0.4.0+stock4.6_amd64.deb \
                ./qsm-pve-direct-standalone_0.4.0+stock4.6_all.deb \
                ./qsm-pve-direct-gateway_0.4.0+stock4.6_all.deb \
                ./qsm-pve-direct-lxc_0.4.0+stock4.6_all.deb

Omit gateway/helper if those profiles are not installed or wanted. QSM service
restarts can interrupt open consoles; reconnect/hard-reload after upgrading.
Running VM buses/configs are preserved and VMs need no reboot for this update.
An enabled gateway stays enabled; an inactive gateway is not enabled by upgrade.

Existing patched shared-platform nodes
-------------------------------------
For the controller fix alone, keep the matched native Proxmox packages and
update just core + matching LXC helper (if installed). Do not add standalone or
gateway expecting an automatic native-viewer conversion: the gateway CLI
rejects the patched pve-manager UI.

A separately planned staged migration may restore a reviewed official
pve-manager UI while retaining the native qemu-server backend for running
shared-display VMs. The standalone compatibility recognizes their dbus-display config and opens QSM
through standalone signalling. It preserves stock VGA serialization and the
existing dbus audio backend instead of adding another private -display.
The core recognizes both shared and private display buses. This transition
was checked without stopping existing VMs.

That is NOT a full removal of native dependencies. Keep the native backend
until each shared-display VM has a planned stopped-VM conversion to the private
standalone display. Removing native qemu-server while a config still contains
dbus-display or driver=dbus can break later starts. The packages do not restore
Proxmox packages, stop VMs or perform this reverse conversion automatically.

Removal
-------
Disable an active gateway first with qsm-pve-direct-gateway disable. Removing
the gateway package also performs its listener rollback. Removing standalone
stops/disables its signalling service; this does not convert VM displays.
The UI uses a reversible version/hash-guarded dpkg-divert of index.html.tpl.
An unreviewed PVE UI update restores stock HTML rather than attempting a patch.
qsm-pve-direct-ui status reports its state. Removing core restores the original
template. Before removing core, disable private Display1 on stopped VMs so later
starts use an ordinary display. Configuration is retained for manual recovery.

Known limits
------------
Still a preview. The configurable maximum remains 0.5..100 Mbit/s (default 8).
Stock4.1 preserves encoder settings and static reference detail when network
feedback changes the working target. It budgets raw pictures before encoding;
insufficient bandwidth lowers motion cadence instead of resetting rate control
and repeatedly refreshing the whole picture. Cheap static pictures can still
retain the configured cadence. Explicit media timestamps preserve playback time.
Fresh receiver estimates bound upward recovery. Large sudden bandwidth drops
can still cause freezes and recovery keyframes. HEVC depends on browser/hardware
support; H.264 is the fallback. See the stock4.2 release for test scope/results.

Stock4.2 input update
--------------------
Reload the PVE page with cache bypass and reopen consoles after upgrading.
Exact typing is the default: one atomic action per source press/repeat, with
no waiting for heartbeat before the first character. Delayed delivery cannot
create extra native repeats. Physical holds and Raw input are available for
games/shortcut editors; these use guest autorepeat and a 200 ms stale-hold
watchdog. Raw forces physical mode. Check Guest first + Exact typing for text
entry if an older browser preference selected Raw. No guest reboot or desktop
reprovisioning is needed. Both the bridge and worker must come from the same
core package; a cached older viewer retains its prior behavior.

Console Settings also provides Esc/Alt+Tab/Ctrl+Alt+Del/Super buttons.
Stock4.4 adds Capture keyboard (full screen) for Firefox/Safari and Chromium;
use the capture button instead of system F11. See the instructions below.

Mouse / trackpad input (stock4.3)
--------------------------------
Exact clicks is the default for ordinary mouse clicks, trackpad taps/clicks
and touchscreen taps. A short click is one complete action sent on release,
including position and modifiers. Moving 4 CSS pixels or holding for 250 ms
starts a physical hold/drag; its motion/release stay ordered until the bridge
acknowledges the release. A 50 ms snapshot renews held state, and 200 ms without
confirmation releases it without re-pressing from stale queued snapshots.
Physical button holds or Raw sends down immediately. Touch hold-to-drag stays
at 500 ms. Both scroll axes work and fractional trackpad deltas accumulate.
Normal double clicks pass; native double-click timing can still be affected
by delivery jitter. Generic virtual input cannot carry original click times.
Reload PVE with cache bypass and reopen consoles. No guest update is needed.

Fullscreen keyboard capture (stock4.4)
-------------------------------------
Reload PVE with cache bypass and reopen the console after updating.
Click Capture keyboard (keyboard/padlock toolbar button or Settings action).
The embedded console enters actual full screen for capture; its existing
page-maximize button still provides in-page enlargement separately.
Capture selects Guest first if Client first was saved, preserving Exact typing
and Exact clicks. Firefox desktop 151+ uses requestFullscreen keyboardLock:
"browser"; Safari uses the same API where supported. Chromium retains
navigator.keyboard.lock() and its permission flow. F11 is not required, so
macOS system assignments need no changes. Click the guest picture for input.

A short Esc is sent to the guest. Hold Esc or click Release keyboard to exit.
Opening Settings, switching to Client first or closing the console releases
capture. Stock4.5 pauses input on host app/tab switching without exiting
fullscreen, then resumes capture inside the existing fullscreen on return.
Held keys/buttons are released on blur; old holds are never restored. Guest first reserves Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Esc;
Raw keeps its existing no-QSM-chord policy. OS-reserved keys are not guaranteed
to reach any web app; use Send to guest buttons where necessary.

Headful Firefox 157 / Linux XTEST / real LXC checks cover ordinary Esc and
browser chords in separate and embedded consoles; complete production UI
smoke checks pass too. No physical macOS/Safari qualification is claimed.

Logical modifiers and host switching (stock4.6)
---------------------------------------------
Use Guest first to respect host modifier remapping (including a macOS
Option/Command swap). Printable positions still follow the guest layout.
Logical Command+Tab / Command+Shift+Tab stay on the Mac and are not sent to
the guest. Logical Option+Tab sends guest Alt+Tab. With swapped host keys,
physical Option now means Command (host switch), and physical Command now
means Option (guest switch). A browser cannot override native macOS app
switching or a third-party global shortcut; do not promise exclusive capture
of those bindings. The existing Alt+Tab Send to guest button stays available.

Outside Raw on macOS, Command is deferred until a guest action, avoiding a
stray guest Alt/Super/menu effect from host Command+Tab. Complete keyboard
and exact mouse actions include logical modifiers. Physical main-key holds,
physical dragging and modifier-wheel input flush deferred Command as needed.
A genuine solo Command tap still sends Super, while blur/host routing cancel
it. Late source presses while unfocused and foreign repeats are ignored.
Raw deliberately uses physical positions/edges and bypasses host-only routing;
use it only when literal physical input is required. Reload PVE without cache
and reopen consoles after upgrading. No guest update is required.
